our shadow tonight goes to a dogged  reporter in the San Francisco Bay Area  whose work has helped to uncover what  really happened in several deadly fires  showing they probably could have and  should have been prevented  just last year in 2018 there was the  state's deadliest and most destructive  fire the campfire reporter Matthias  Gaffney known as T in the newsroom was  determined to find out the story of what  happened using a database that archives  police and fire frequencies and I just  started listening right around the time  that they believe the fire started and  sure enough I heard that a dispatcher  get a call okay cross under the  high-tech power lines there's a possible  power line hazard and they basically  reported that there were wires down  underneath transmission lines  and this is right at the moment that the  campfires believed was started and right  in the area that the campfire was  believed to have started 1026 is falling  apart right down so that was a real big  break in finding out what may be the  cause for this fire the evidence  pointing to the state giant PG&amp;E utility  key Genie just now I said that they are  probably at fault with their equipment  having a malfunction and so that was you  know just a few days after the camp fire  that we were able to narrow that down  and and show that PG&amp;E very well could  be at fault and beyond the equipment  malfunction Gaffney determined that PG&amp;E  if it followed its own guidelines should  have shut down the power in such high  risk fire conditions most of those  criteria were actually met prior to the  camp fire yet for some reason they  decided to keep the power on PG&amp;E now  has declared bankruptcy saying it  expects to face massive lawsuits two  years earlier in another deadly fire  Gaffney was just as adept in disproving  the notion that city officials in  Oakland were unaware of potentially  dangerous conditions at a huge warehouse  called the ship where dozens of artists  and musicians had taken up residence we  wound up getting and we found that  police officers routinely were  responding there and when they responded  there we found a couple things out from  their body cam footage one was that the  the lead tenant of that warehouse was  saying that no one lived inside the the  warehouse which was not true and then  but we also found that in one instance  two police officers respond to a call  for service and you see them walking  through this maze inside the warehouse  and you can hear one of the officers say  something to the effect of all would  take is one match in this place would go  up  thirty-six people died in the fire two  of the buildings principle tenants have  been charged criminally in both the city  and the Landlord are facing huge  lawsuits Gaffney's reporting on the ship  fire earned him and his fellow reporters  a Pulitzer Prize and two weeks ago he  joined the San Francisco Chronicle after  being named the journalist of the year  in Northern California by the Society of  Professional Journalists I'm born and  raised in the Bay Area here and I have a  you know family I find it really  impactful to kind of do reporting that  immediately impacts my community and you  know the people I've grown up with and I  love and respect so it's really pretty  rewarding so our shout-out tonight to  Matthias Gaffney that's our program  thanks for joining us good night
 

